\title{\uppercase{\bf Forecasting CO$_2$ Flux at Time-Interval Day-Night Levels in the Midwest}}

\author{\textsc{Sepideh Mosaferi} \\\textit{Department of Statistics, Iowa State University}}


\setstretch{1.0}

\twocolumn[
\begin{@twocolumnfalse}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}

\noindent We are motivated by a data set related to the CO$_2$ flux measurements obtained from an eddy covariance device which are often non-stationary in space and time and require careful modeling to effectively predict or forecast the amount of CO$_2$ flux at the level of interested areas and/or state indicators identified by time.
For this particular project, we access the CO$_2$ measurements over corn-crop areas in Midwest, which is pretty rich in length of time with different seasonal and day/night time pattern. Based on this motivation, we allow a site-specific time series model as the locations are not available in such a way to effectively consider time as a state indicator and to propose a Bayesain model to predict and eventually forecast the aggregated amount of CO$_2$ flux at time interval levels.
We evaluate the point uncertainty of predicted values and the overall uncertainty of our proposd model via the standard mean squared error and desirable plots. Finally, the ten step ahead of forecasting values will be provided.   

\vspace{0.25cm}
\noindent KEY WORDS and PHRASES: Dynamic Linear Model; Eddy-Covariance; Bayesian Time Modeling; State Modeling; MCMC.
\end{abstract}
\end{@twocolumnfalse}

]

\vspace{1cm}

\section{Introduction} \label{sec:introduction}
We are motivated by a data set that occasionally exist in the environmental studies such as moisture flux across an air-water interface in meterology, wind process, etc. These set of data typically contain measurement errors and/or biases as the devices used for measuring the amounts are not perfect.
We are primarly interested in the CO$_2$ flux measurements which can be obtained from different devices. One of these devices called \textit{eddy-covariance}, and it presents the amount of CO$_2$ flux as the mean
covariance between deviations in instantaneous vertical wind speed and density of CO$_2$ in the air.
A positive measure corresponds to an increased amount of CO$_2$ in the air while a negative measure corresponds to a decreased amount. 

Usually CO$_2$ flux measures are at the micro-levels, so-called fine scales in the spatial terminology. While these measurements do not exist for all fine scales nested in the large regions, one might be interested in predicting their amounts for aggregated time intervals  as our state indicator) through an appropriate model. Therefore, we propose an effective model in which we combine the adjacent data fine points based upon the day-night time stamp in the Summer 2015 to produce a large scale state levels\footnote{This term is related to the Dynamic Linear System and will be defined further.} and predict the amount of CO$_2$ at these levels.
The available data could be variable in time, and a hierarchical Bayesian state dynamic model could be appropriate for this data set.
We can also reflect the uncertainty of model into the choice of prior specification (e.g., \cite{wikle1998hierarchical} and \cite{wikle2001spatiotemporal}).

We specify the observed measures of CO$_2$ flux in the first stage of the proposed hierarchical model. In the second stage of the model, we allow state-specific time series model and incorporate the time dynamic terms to generate temporal dependencies and/or interactions (if needed). The reminder stages of the hierarchical model are related to completing the Bayesian formulation and specifying the priors on un-known parameters. We finally evaluate the proposed model and predicted values with appropriate measures of uncertainty such as mean squared-error, etc. 
The rest of the report proceeds as follows. In Section \ref{sec:data}, we explain the available data and conduct some primary data analysis to decide the detailed parts of the model. In Section \ref{sec:model}, we propose the set-up of our hierarchial Bayesian model. Section \ref{sec:result} contains our results and discussion is given in Section \ref{sec:discus}. Some improvements and future works will be provided in Section \ref{sec:future}, and supplementary Figures and Tables will be defered to the Appendices. 

\section{Description of Data Set and Data Analysis} \label{sec:data}
In this section, we first introduce our available data and some of our main goals. We later aggregate the data set over time intervals effectively to produce desirable state indicators and provide some data analysis. 
As we mentioned earlier, our data set is related to the CO$_2$ flux measures at the fine levels over corn yields.
The corn data set is from 2006 until 2015 and the amounts have been measured per every 15 minutes starting from the midnight 24hrs of day. 

The length of data set is 350,592 which reduces to 92,624 after removing the missing values [NAs] \footnote{We made an assumption in which the mechanism of missingness is completely at random and deletion of NAs does not result into a loss of information.}. 
We should emphasize that we do not access the locations of the data set for any possible space model consideration. As the length of the data set is very large and some of the seasonal and yearly patterns repeated over time, we decide to specifically focus on the data from summer 2015 depicted on Figure \ref{figure1} with the day/night time recognition box-plot as well.

\begin{figure}[ht] 
\centering   %centering figure
\includegraphics[width=0.4 \textwidth]{Explanatory.pdf} 
\caption{Panel A) The kernel density plot of CO$_2$ for Summer 2015. The data set is highly left-skewed and majority of values are in the negative range (around $\%$70 of the data set). Panel B) Boxplot of CO$_2$ for day-night time. Observe that the values during the day time cover a wide range compared to the night time, which could be a reason that day and night CO$_2$ flux have different patterns.}
\label{figure1}
\end{figure}

Based on Figure \ref{figure1}, we can see the CO$_2$ flux has a left skewed distribution with a very sharp peak (kurtosis>3). The overall range of CO$_2$ is [-3.06,1.28]. In addition, there is a clear dis-similarity between the patterns of data during day time and night time. Therefore, it might be desirable to aggregate the data set in the form of regular average at the day/night level per day to reflect their patterns in the state modeling. As a result, we have 107 points as the information have not been recorded for some of the days. The summary of the aggregated points are given in Table \ref{table1}.
In addition, more details related to the data set is given on Figure \ref{figure2}. From Figure \ref{figure2}, we can conclude that the data is not highly correlated during the aggregated course of time and the normality assumption does not hold. We applied a Box-Cox transformation on the data and found the value of $\lambda=-0.023$ seems reasonable and makes the data approximately normal. The Box-Cox transformation can be defined as follows:

\begin{equation*}
y(\lambda)= \left\{
  \begin{array}{@{}ll@{}}
    \frac{y^{\lambda}-1}{\lambda} & \qquad if \quad \lambda \neq 0\\
    log(\lambda) & \qquad if \quad \lambda = 0
  \end{array}\right.
\end{equation*}
in which we apply the first component of the above transformation on the data set.

Based on our further investigation, the amount of CO$_2$ changes over day and night and allowed us to fit a suitable dynamic linear model.
The main purpose of this research is evaluating the Eddy-Covariance device for forecasting future amounts of CO$_2$ flux measures. This helps scientists to decide whether the device is accurate enough and whether there is a need for more investments in the corp yields to increase the amount of production.
