\section{Dynamic Linear Model} \label{sec:model}

Consider a time series ($Y_t$)_{t \geq 1}$, in which we assume there is an un-observable Markov chain ($\theta_t)$, called the state process, and $Y_t$ is a measurement of $\theta_t$ containing error.
Our dynamic linear model could be specified as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\left\{
  \begin{array}{@{}ll@{}}
    y_t=F_t \theta_t+\nu_t, & \nu_t \stackrel{iid}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,V_t)\\
    \theta_t=G_t\theta_{t-1}+w_t, & w_t \stackrel{iid}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,W_t)
  \end{array}\right.
\end{equation*}
for $t=1,...,n$ where $n=107$. We consider a prior distribution for $\theta_0$,
\begin{equation*}
\theta_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(m_0,C_0).
\end{equation*}
in which $m_0$ and $C_0$ are known equal to one and we follow the first order linear trend mode. Let's define:
\begin{equation*}
V=\sigma^2, \qquad W=\sigma^2_{\beta},
\end{equation*}
followed by the notations proposed by \cite{petris}. The unknown parameters are $\theta$\footnote{We call $\theta$ states.} and $\psi=(\sigma^2,\sigma^2_{\beta})^t$. To estimate the un-knowns, we use two methods: 1. Frequentist approach by considering the MLEs; 2. Bayesian approach. The main focus of this manuscript is centered around the second technique.

We can parameterize the variances in the log-scale to ensure positivity, and for simplicity, we assume both the priors of $\sigma^2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$ are independent following an inverse-gamma distribution with shape and scale parameters equal to 1:  
\begin{equation*}
\sigma^2 \sim \textit{inv-gamma}(1,1); \qquad \sigma^2_{\beta} \sim \textit{inv-gamma}(1,1).
\end{equation*}
The joint posterior distribution is:
\begin{equation*}
\pi(\theta_s,\psi|y_{1:t})=\pi(\theta_s|\psi,y_{1:t}) \pi(\psi|y_{1:t}).
\end{equation*}

After some algebra, we can conclude the following full conditionals for $\sigma^2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$:
$$
\pi[\sigma^2|\textit{rest}] \sim \textit{inv-gamma}(1+\frac{T}{2},1+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\nu_t^2),
$$
where $\nu_t=Y_t-F\theta_t$, and
$$
\pi[\sigma^2_{\beta}|\textit{rest}] \sim \textit{inv-gamma}(1+\frac{T}{2},1+\frac{1}{2}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\omega_t^2),
$$
where $\omega_t=\theta_t-G\theta_{t-1}.$
In addition, the unknown parameters $[\theta|\textit{rest}]$ can be accomplished through 
\newline \texttt{dlm::dlmBSample} and \texttt{dlm::dlmFilter} in R.

Here, we consider the problems of filtering (when $s = t$), smoothing ($s < t$), and ten-step ahead of forecasting for the states $\theta_{t+k} (k=1,...,10)$. In the following subsection, we provide some details related to the Bayes and model checking.

\subsection{Bayesian Model Checking} \label{sec:Bayesiancheck}
We have conducted a Gibbs sampler to estimate the unknown parameters. The size of the chain is $10,000$ in which we disgard the first $500$ values as burn-in. The histograms for the posteriors of $\sigma^_2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$ have been provided in Figure \ref{figure3}. Observe that posteriors mainly follow the data and priors do not have any effects on the posteriors\footnote{We have considered the MLEs as the initial values of Gibbs sampler.}. 

\begin{figure}[ht] 
\centering   %centering figure
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{HistogramPosterior.pdf} 
\caption{Histogram for the posterior of unknown parameters. Observe in both panels, the histograms mainly follow the data sets and the priors (indicated by red color) do not have essential effects on the posteriors.}
\label{figure3}
\end{figure}

The ACF and PACF of $\sigma^2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$ from Figure \ref{figure8} in Appendix \ref{app:fig} do not show significant correlations in the ramained part of the chain. Trace plots and density plots for $\sigma^2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$ are also provided in Panel A of Figure \ref{figure10} (See Appendix \ref{app:fig}). The covergency plots for the first two and last two states are given in Panel B of Figure \ref{figure10} (See Appendix \ref{app:fig}). All the results show no lack of convergence. The Geweke's plots and the Z-scores for some of unknown parameters are given in Figure \ref{figure9} and Table \ref{table3}, respectively, which confirm the above-mentioned results. Summary of the unknown parameters $\sigma^2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$ are given in Table \ref{table2}.


