\section{Results and Model Evaluation} \label{sec:result}
In this Section, we give the main results based on the filtered values ($s=t$), smoothed values ($s<t$) and 10 step ahead forecasting. Specifically forecasting is important to us to know whether our model is appropriate enough for measuring the amount of CO$_2$ in the future. 

\subsection{Kalman Filter Values} \label{sec:filter}
Based on the proposed DLM, we can define:
\begin{equation*}
\theta_{t-1}|y_{1:t-1} \sim \mathcal{N}(m_{t-1},C_{t-1}),
\end{equation*}
then the filtering distribution of $\theta_t$ given $y_{1:t}$ is Gaussian, with parameters:
\begin{equation*}
m_t=E(\theta_t|y_{1:t})=a_t+R_tF^t_tQ^{-1}_te_t,

\newline
C_t=Var(\theta_t|y_{1:t})=R_t-R_tF^t_tQ^{-1}_tF_tR_t.
\end{equation*}

\vspace{0.2cm}
\noindent We refer an interested reader to \cite{petris} in order to obtain the details related to the above-mentioned equations. We have given the filtered values in Figure \ref{figure4}. In Figure \ref{figure4}, we have also provided the maximum likelihood inference in which we estimated the un-known parameters based on the profile likelihood. We can see the Bayesian inference looks more appropriate as its overall MSE calculated from $\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{Y}-\boldsymbol{\theta})^t(\boldsymbol{Y}-\boldsymbol{\theta})]$ is lower than the MLE one. 

\begin{figure}[ht] 
\centering   %centering figure
\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{filter.pdf} 
\caption{Time series plot of observations and filtered values. Filtered values are obtained based on the MLE and Bayesian techniques. Observe from the Bayesian technique, we can have less amount of MSE.}
\label{figure4}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Smoothing Values} \label{sec:smooth}
A backward recursive algorithm can be used to compute the conditional distributions of $\theta_t$ given $y_{1:T}$ for any $t<T$, starting from the filtering distribution $\pi(\theta_T|y_{1:T})$ and estimating backward all the states $\theta$'s. The smoothing distributions of $\theta_t$ given $y_{1:T}$ can be written as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\pi(\theta_t|y_{1:T})=\pi(\theta_t|y_{1:t}) \int \frac{\pi(\theta_{t+1}|\theta_{t})}{\pi(\theta_{t+1}|y_{1:T})}\pi(\theta_{t+1}|y_{1:T})d\theta_{t+1}.
\end{equation*}
The smoothed values and the $\%95$ confidence bands are given in Figure \ref{figure5}. We have considered both MLE and Bayesian inference for estimating the unknown parameter vector. The results show that the posterior intervals for the smoothed values are very wider rather than the MLE one as it was expected.

\begin{figure}[ht] 
\centering   %centering figure
\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{Smoothedvalue.pdf} 
\caption{Time-series plot of observations and smoothed values. Smoothed values are computed based on the MLE and Bayesian technique. The posterior confidence band is extremly wider than the MLE one.}
\label{figure5}
\end{figure}

In the rest of the this section, we compare the results of filtered values and smoothed values based on the Bayesian inference. The results have been depicted in Figure \ref{figure6}. As we can see, the results show that the smoothed values have greater overall amount of MSEs.

\begin{figure}[ht] 
\centering   %centering figure
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{SmoothFilterBayes.pdf} 
\caption{Timeseries plot of observations including the filtered and smoothed values, in which both computed from the Bayesian technique. We can observe that the filtered values have less amount of overall MSE compared to the smoothed values.}
\label{figure6}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Ten-Step Ahead Forecasting} \label{sec:forecast}
Here, we are interested in the forecasting future values of the observations $Y_{t+k}$. This is mainly interesting as it could help scientists to decide whether the device can measure the values of CO$_2$ accurately and the future values follow the proposed model. On the other hand, it is a tool for checking the performance of the model. For the one-step ahead, we can use the formula $f_t=\mathbb{E}(Y_t|y_{1:t-1})$ in R from the function \texttt{dlmFilter}. In many applications, we are interested in looking further future and observe the behavior of series for $k$ step ahead, however, the level of uncertainty increases as we move further (i.e. as the amount of $k$ increases).

The $k$-steps-ahead forecast distributions of the state and observation are as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\pi(\theta_{t+k}|y_{1:t})=\int \pi(\theta_{t+k}|\theta_{t+k-1})\pi(\theta_{t+k-1}|y_{1:t}) d\theta_{t+k-1},
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\pi(y_{t+k}|y_{1:t})=\int \pi(y_{t+k}|\theta_{t+k}) \pi(\theta_{t+k}|y_{1:t}) d\theta_{t+k-1}.
\end{equation*}

As we mentioned earlier, our main purpose is related to the forecasting the future observations. The results for 10-step ahead of forecasting is given in Figure \ref{figure7}. From this Figure, we can see that as the amount of $k$ increases, the amount of uncertainty increases. Therefore, the proposed model might not quite appropriate for forecasting very far away values.

\begin{figure}[ht] 
\centering   %centering figure
\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{TenstepaheadF.pdf} 
\caption{Ten step ahead forecasting. The amount of uncertainty for each point is given under the plot. Observe as $k$ increases, the amount of uncertainty in terms of variance increases and we can less rely on the model for forecasting the future observations.}
\label{figure7}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion} \label{sec:discus}
we consider a Gaussian dynamic linear model to forecast the amount of CO$_2$ for the future 10 obs (i.e. 5 days and 5 nights). Based on the results, the model might not be appropriate enough for the future forecasting which is important for farmers and scientistis in terms of investment. It might be desirable to extend the model to the some sort of non-Gaussian case before considering a $\lambda$ in the Box-Cox transformation. This might also helps to consider some sort of non-linearity in the model. Looking at the results further, we can see that the distribution of unknown parameters $\sigma^2$ and $\sigma^2_{\beta}$ are quite close and both are not affected by the priors. Therefore, it might be interesting to consider subjective priors based on the data sets that we have from previous years. We can draw plots of information from prevoius summer to see which kind of priors are suitable.

\section{Future Research} \label{sec:future}
As we mentioned in the Introduction, we access the large set of data from 2006-2015. Some of the values of CO$_2$ flux in the previous years measured differently through other devices and not with the Eddy Covariance. From part of our future research, we are interested in comparison of the accuracy of devices in order to know which one could give us better predicetd and forecasting values. This could help scientists to decide which device has better performance to be used more generally. In addition, it could be interesting to provide some longitudinal study for providing an effective comparison and proposing a time-series model based containing all patterns of years and seasons. However, this could be subject of more computational burden.  

In addition, it may be of interest to consider some measurement error terms in the model which could reflect the errors in the measuring the CO$_2$ through the device and we can put in the second stage of our dynamic hierarchical model. This could brings more variations in the final stage of predicted values, however. As we mentioned previously, the measures are at the fine levels nested in the coarse level (larger scales than the fine scales in the spatial terminology). We could define some sort of multi-scale modeling to predict the amount of CO$_2$ based on modeling the both fine and coarse levels. 


